---
const features = [
  {
    icon: '🖥️',
    color: 'var(--parrot)',
    title: 'Runs entirely on your machine',
    body: 'Local recognizers turn speech into text on-device: Parakeet for English, Whisper for ten more languages. No cloud, no API keys. Pull the Wi-Fi and it still works.',
  },
  {
    icon: '🎯',
    color: 'var(--fox)',
    title: 'Types into the right place',
    body: 'It leases the field under your cursor and checks it is still there before pasting once. If focus moved, the text parks safely in your Outbox instead of the wrong box.',
  },
  {
    icon: '⚡',
    color: 'var(--bear)',
    title: 'Built not to wait',
    body: 'Models stay warm and long sentences are recognized while you are still talking, so only the last stretch is left to decode when you let go of the key. We have not published a latency measurement yet.',
  },
  {
    icon: '✍️',
    color: 'var(--owl)',
    title: 'Cleans up by the rules',
    body: 'Cleanup removes filler and follows spoken structure. One rule waits for you to switch it on: "Tuesday, actually Wednesday" then keeps only Wednesday. It is off by default because "I thought it was Tuesday, actually Wednesday" would lose Tuesday too. App names and your dictionary can steer recognition but never replace a word you said, and the optional cleanup model may only make edits it can prove.',
  },
  {
    icon: '📓',
    color: 'var(--cat)',
    title: 'Learns your words, not your life',
    body: 'A word you fix the same way twice becomes a small, local rule you can read and forget. It never maps one everyday word to another or flips a negation, and there is no cloud transcript following you around.',
  },
  {
    icon: '🛟',
    color: 'var(--teal)',
    title: 'Nothing gets lost',
    body: 'Faithful fallbacks, exact-once insertion, and a recoverable Outbox mean a bad moment never eats your sentence.',
  },
  {
    icon: '⏪',
    color: 'var(--pig)',
    title: 'Talk first, decide after',
    body: 'Turn on Flight Recorder and the microphone keeps the last twenty seconds in memory, never on disk. Say the thing, realise you wanted it, tap the key and keep it.',
  },
  {
    icon: '🎚️',
    color: 'var(--wolf)',
    title: 'Six ways to say it',
    body: 'Hold a modifier with your key to dictate code with real punctuation or run an editing command. Compose, reply, and edit-the-selection need the optional cleanup model (about 3.4 GB) and English; without it they change nothing and tell you so.',
  },
  {
    icon: '🔍',
    color: 'var(--tiger)',
    title: 'Show your work',
    body: 'Every result can explain itself — what it protected, which edits it accepted or refused, what it was unsure about. No transcript dossier required.',
  },
];
---

<section class="band wrap" id="features">
  <div class="sec-head">
    <span class="kicker">Why it feels different</span>
    <h2>An interface between thought and software.</h2>
    <p>Not another transcript box. Every part is built to earn your trust with what you actually said.</p>
    <p class="sec-note">
      Described as they work on a Mac with a default install: Parakeet and Whisper Tiny. The larger
      Whisper model and the cleanup model are optional downloads. Windows shares capture,
      recognition, cleanup, snippets and tones, but not the verified insertion transaction, the
      Outbox, correction learning, or the results view — see
      <a href="/docs/comparison">how it compares</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="features">
    {
      features.map((f) => (
        <article class="feature" data-jelly>
          <div class="badge" style={`background:${f.color}`}>{f.icon}</div>
          <h3>{f.title}</h3>
          <p>{f.body}</p>
        </article>
      ))
    }
  </div>
</section>
